{% section-card tone="intro" %}
## What is an image captioning system?

An image captioning system turns visual evidence into a short natural-language description. It combines a vision encoder, which recognizes objects and relationships, with a language model, which expresses that evidence as a caption. The result supports screen readers, image search, cataloging, and content workflows.

The central invariant is simple: **describe what the image supports; do not turn a plausible guess into a fact**. A fluent caption can still be wrong, biased, or harmful. For this interview, design a service for **100 million images per day**, a **5,000 request/s peak**, an interactive target under **500 ms at p95**, and batch processing for work that does not need an immediate answer.

{% concept-grid columns="3" %}
{% concept-card title="Ground every claim" eyebrow="Correctness" icon="shield" tone="blue" %}
Objects, text, actions, and relationships need visible or verified evidence. Omit uncertain details instead of inventing them.
{% /concept-card %}
{% concept-card title="Route by consequence" eyebrow="Product" icon="route" tone="violet" %}
Accessibility, search metadata, social copy, and regulated imagery require different latency, model, and review policies.
{% /concept-card %}
{% concept-card title="A partial caption is valid" eyebrow="Degraded mode" icon="activity" tone="green" %}
When OCR, grounding, or capacity is weak, return a conservative description, queue review, or return no caption.
{% /concept-card %}
{% /concept-grid %}
{% /section-card %}

{% accordion defaultOpen="clarifying" %}

{% accordion-item id="clarifying" title="1. Clarify Requirements and Guardrails" %}


### Separate the user journeys

The word "caption" hides several products. Ask which one is being built before choosing a model or metric.

- **Accessibility alt text**
    - Describe the information a screen-reader user needs to understand the image in context.
    - Prefer factual completeness and predictable wording over creativity.
- **Search and catalog metadata**
    - Extract objects, attributes, and text that improve retrieval.
    - Structured tags may matter more than a polished sentence.
- **Social assistance**
    - Suggest editable copy, but never publish it without an explicit user action.
    - Brand voice is a soft preference; safety and grounding remain hard gates.
- **Regulated imagery**
    - Route medical, legal, biometric, or safety-critical images to specialized policy and human review.
    - Do not present a general captioner as a diagnostic system.

### State the service promises

**Functional requirements**

- Accept common image formats and an optional language, use-case, and style policy.
- Return a caption, grounded entities, confidence metadata, and a review status.
- Support at least 25 languages without translating unsupported claims into new certainty.
- Let authorized users correct captions and send those corrections into a governed feedback pipeline.

**Non-functional requirements**

- Meet 500 ms p95 for the interactive tier and process the remaining workload asynchronously.
- Keep raw images private, encrypted, region-aware, and short-lived by default.
- Measure quality by use case, language, image type, and user-impact slice.
- Degrade safely during model, OCR, moderation, or regional failure.

### Define the first-version boundary

- No diagnosis, identity inference, emotion labeling, or protected-attribute guessing.
- No promise that every image receives a useful caption.
- No permanent image retention unless a separate product policy requires it.
- No open-ended creative generation on the accessibility path.

{% /accordion-item %}

{% accordion-item id="calculations" title="2. Estimate Traffic, Compute, and Latency" %}


### Convert daily volume into capacity

Start with replaceable assumptions:

1. Average request rate = 100M / 86,400 = about **1,157 images/s**.
2. A 4.3x daily peak produces about **5,000 images/s**.
3. If 30% is interactive, the immediate path receives **1,500 images/s** at peak.
4. At a 2 MB average image size, ingress is roughly **200 TB/day** before compression, deduplication, or retention policy.

{% metric-strip %}
{% metric value="100M/day" label="Images" detail="A planning assumption, not a production claim." tone="blue" /%}
{% metric value="5K/s" label="Peak requests" detail="About 4.3 times the daily average." tone="cyan" /%}
{% metric value="30%" label="Interactive share" detail="The rest can use efficient batch queues." tone="violet" /%}
{% metric value="500 ms" label="Interactive p95" detail="Includes upload, preprocessing, inference, checks, and response." tone="green" /%}
{% /metric-strip %}

### Size from measured service capacity

Do not derive accelerator count from model parameter count. Benchmark a complete serving replica with the intended image resolution, precision, batching policy, and output length. Then divide peak routed traffic by measured requests per second and add regional-failure and deployment headroom.

### Make the capacity decision

{% interactive-block id="practice/image-captioning-capacity-lab" /%}

The important interview move is to connect the product split to the fleet: moving work from batch to interactive changes both replica demand and queueing risk, even though daily image volume stays constant.

{% /accordion-item %}

{% accordion-item id="architecture" title="3. Draw the High-Level Architecture" %}


### Keep policy around the model

{% system-flow title="Grounded caption request path" caption="The generator proposes language; independent evidence and policy checks decide whether that language may leave the service." %}
{% system-node title="Upload edge" eyebrow="Protect" icon="globe" tone="blue" %}
Authenticate, enforce size and format limits, scan the file, and write a short-lived object.
{% /system-node %}
{% system-node title="Image router" eyebrow="Classify" icon="route" tone="cyan" %}
Detect domain, use case, language, complexity, and whether OCR or specialist review is required.
{% /system-node %}
{% system-node title="Vision-language tier" eyebrow="Generate" icon="sparkles" tone="violet" %}
Encode visual regions and decode one or more caption candidates under a bounded token budget.
{% /system-node %}
{% system-node title="Grounding gate" eyebrow="Verify" icon="shield" tone="amber" %}
Compare claims with detected regions, OCR evidence, sensitive-attribute policy, and confidence thresholds.
{% /system-node %}
{% system-node title="Caption response" eyebrow="Deliver" icon="check" tone="green" %}
Return the safe caption and evidence metadata; emit audit and feedback events asynchronously.
{% /system-node %}
{% /system-flow %}

### Use tiers with explicit responsibilities

- **On-device or compact tier:** private, low-latency captions for common photographs; limited vocabulary and quality.
- **Interactive cloud tier:** the default path for diverse images under the 500 ms budget.
- **Batch quality tier:** larger model, richer decoding, and better accelerator utilization for libraries and re-indexing.
- **Specialist route:** OCR-heavy charts, documents, medical images, and other domains with different evidence and review rules.

### Separate synchronous and asynchronous work

The request path performs only work needed to make a release decision. Dataset curation, aggregate analytics, feedback processing, retraining, and bulk re-captioning consume events from durable queues. They must not extend an interactive response deadline.

{% /accordion-item %}

{% accordion-item id="deep-dive" title="4. Design the Model, Data, and Grounding Layers" %}


### Frame the ML task

- **Input:** decoded image regions plus optional locale, use-case, and permitted context.
- **Output:** a bounded caption and claim-level evidence metadata.
- **Training objective:** next-token prediction plus image-text alignment and preference signals.
- **Serving objective:** useful grounded captions per unit cost under a latency and policy budget.

### Build the model in three responsibilities

{% process-flow %}
{% process-step number="1" title="Encode visual evidence" label="Perception" %}
Create patch or region features while preserving objects, spatial relationships, and text-bearing areas.
{% /process-step %}
{% process-step number="2" title="Generate candidates" label="Language" %}
Decode concise alternatives conditioned on use case and language without granting the decoder factual authority.
{% /process-step %}
{% process-step number="3" title="Align claims to regions" label="Grounding" %}
Map nouns, attributes, actions, and OCR-derived text back to evidence and attach confidence.
{% /process-step %}
{% process-step number="4" title="Apply release policy" label="Decision" %}
Remove unsupported details, hedge genuine uncertainty, or route the image to review.
{% /process-step %}
{% /process-flow %}

### Curate data by purpose

- Use licensed, consented, and documented image-caption pairs with provenance and deletion support.
- Balance geography, language, skin tone, disability representation, indoor and outdoor scenes, and image quality.
- Add domain sets for charts, screenshots, artwork, documents, and other supported routes.
- Include hard negatives where a fluent sentence mentions an object or relationship that is absent.
- Keep a human-written, slice-balanced evaluation set outside the training and tuning loop.

### Avoid metric-shaped shortcuts

Reference overlap rewards captions that resemble annotator wording, but valid captions can differ. Conversely, a sentence can score well while hallucinating a detail. Use BLEU, CIDEr, and embedding similarity as diagnostics, then pair them with claim grounding, human usefulness, and safety evaluation.

{% /accordion-item %}

{% accordion-item id="evaluation" title="5. Define Evaluation and Release Gates" %}


### Evaluate claims, not only sentences

**Offline quality**

- Claim precision: the fraction of mentioned objects, attributes, actions, and relationships supported by evidence.
- Coverage: whether important visible information for the use case is omitted.
- Human usefulness: factuality, clarity, relevance, and accessibility value.
- Language quality: fluency and terminology, measured separately from factual grounding.

**Production behavior**

- p50, p95, and p99 latency by route, image size, region, and model tier.
- Caption acceptance, correction, deletion, and fallback rates by use case.
- OCR disagreement, unsupported-claim rate, sensitive-inference blocks, and review volume.
- Cost per released caption, not merely cost per attempted inference.

**Hard launch guardrails**

- No cross-tenant image or caption leakage.
- No increase in unsupported sensitive attributes.
- No critical regression for a language, image domain, or accessibility slice.
- Regulated routes cannot bypass their required human-review policy.

### Inspect a caption before releasing it

{% interactive-block id="practice/image-captioning-grounding-gate" /%}

Automatic text similarity cannot make this decision. The release gate needs claim-level evidence and a use-case policy that treats omission, uncertainty, and regulated content differently.

{% /accordion-item %}

{% accordion-item id="operations" title="6. Operate, Retrain, and Govern the System" %}


### Observe the whole decision path

- **Input health:** decode failures, image size, domain mix, OCR rate, corruption, and policy rejects.
- **Serving health:** queue age, batch size, accelerator utilization, stage latency, timeout, and fallback rate.
- **Output health:** unsupported claims, omission, language mismatch, sensitive-inference blocks, and review outcomes.
- **Data health:** source provenance, consent, deletion, drift, duplicate rate, and evaluation-slice coverage.

### Turn feedback into governed labels

User edits are valuable but ambiguous: a rewrite may reflect tone rather than factual error. Store the original claim-evidence record, correction category, model version, and policy version. Sample corrections for trained review, deduplicate near-identical images, and prevent automatically generated captions from silently becoming trusted training labels.

### Promote artifacts like code

1. Validate data lineage and training configuration.
2. Run deterministic, slice, adversarial, and privacy tests.
3. Shadow on production-shaped traffic without returning output.
4. Canary by stable user or tenant assignment with stop conditions.
5. Retain the previous model, tokenizer, and policy bundle for rollback.

{% /accordion-item %}

{% accordion-item id="trade-offs" title="7. Plan Scalability, Reliability, and Trade-offs" %}


### Degrade in a deliberate order

{% process-flow %}
{% process-step number="1" title="Reject invalid work" label="Protect" %}
Stop oversized, corrupted, unsupported, or unauthorized uploads before expensive inference.
{% /process-step %}
{% process-step number="2" title="Route to a compact tier" label="Capacity" %}
Reduce resolution, decoding width, or model size while preserving grounding checks.
{% /process-step %}
{% process-step number="3" title="Move eligible work to batch" label="Queue" %}
Return a job status for catalog workloads that do not require an immediate caption.
{% /process-step %}
{% process-step number="4" title="Return no caption" label="Safety" %}
Do not bypass evidence or policy checks to make an availability graph look healthy.
{% /process-step %}
{% /process-flow %}

### Name the trade-offs

- **Resolution versus latency:** larger images preserve small objects and text but raise preprocessing and encoder cost.
- **Beam width versus throughput:** more candidates may improve wording while multiplying decoder work.
- **One model versus specialist routes:** a unified fleet is simpler; specialists can improve difficult domains but add routing and governance complexity.
- **Creative detail versus factual trust:** descriptive variety helps social copy but is dangerous for accessibility and search metadata.
- **Cross-region failover versus privacy:** moving work may restore capacity but can violate residency or consent boundaries.

### Handle failure by user consequence

- If OCR is unavailable, omit text-dependent claims or send the image to review.
- If the grounding service is unavailable, do not release freshly generated captions automatically.
- If one region fails, route only requests permitted to move and batch the rest.
- If the new model regresses a critical slice, roll back the full model-policy bundle rather than adjusting thresholds blindly.

{% /accordion-item %}

{% accordion-item id="wrap-up" title="8. Summarize Risks and Extensions" %}


### Present the design in 90 seconds

1. Classify each request by product use case, domain, latency, privacy, and review policy.
2. Route interactive and batch work to separately optimized vision-language tiers.
3. Keep generation inside an evidence gate that checks claims, OCR, sensitive inference, and uncertainty.
4. Return a conservative caption, review status, or no caption when the service cannot protect the invariant.
5. Launch only when usefulness improves across slices and every hard grounding, safety, privacy, and latency gate passes.

### Call out unresolved risks

- Small or occluded objects may be important even when the model assigns low confidence.
- Human captions contain cultural assumptions and omissions that training can amplify.
- OCR can faithfully extract private or harmful text that should not be repeated.
- Corrections and acceptance signals favor active users and common languages.
- Domain routing mistakes can send specialist images through an unsafe general policy.

### Offer credible extensions

- Generate interactive region-to-phrase explanations for editors and auditors.
- Add document, chart, and screenshot routes with layout-aware OCR.
- Support on-device private captions with cloud escalation only after consent.
- Produce multiple caption lengths for screen readers, search indexes, and editorial workflows.
- Re-caption stored libraries after a model upgrade through versioned, reversible batch jobs.

{% /accordion-item %}

{% /accordion %}

